{
  "step1_summary": "- Chief complaint: Right knee pain with clicking for ~2 weeks; reduced running tolerance. Occasional tingling in right toes (one episode after a run).\n- Symptoms (onset/duration/severity/aggravating/alleviating): Symptoms began ~2 weeks ago after increasing running mileage while training for a marathon (started longer runs ~1 month ago). Pain typically begins ~20 minutes into a run (~2.5–3 miles). Pain provokes clicking with flexion/extension (noticed during gym/abs work and running). Initial mild swelling after first long run resolved with ice. Currently pain not constant and no pain at rest most of the time. Aggravated by running and certain movements (straightening from flexed knee); improved with ice and rest. Tingling in toes was transient and resolved.\n- Past medical history: Generally well. Prior LASIK. Previous ER visit for presumed kidney stone / severe UTI in past. Prior ice-hockey fall months ago causing a large bruise (no documented fracture/ligament repair).\n- Medications / allergies: Vitamin D, melatonin, ibuprofen/NSAIDs (occasional), Aleve (naproxen?) occasionally, occasional Xanax, smokes cannabis (a joint). No medication allergies documented.\n- Family / social history: Active in sports (running, ice hockey). Training for marathon. Lives in London. No family history of rheumatologic disease documented. Occasional cannabis use.\n- Vital signs / exam findings: No formal vitals recorded. On video assessment: full range of motion, no current swelling, no warmth, no locking, no giving-way reported, able to walk and use stairs. Patient reports a transient episode of mild swelling previously and a one-time tingling in right toes.\n- Labs / imaging: None performed or mentioned.",
  "step2_vindicate": {
    "V": [
      "Deep vein thrombosis (DVT): Possible when unilateral leg symptoms and swelling/induration occur; marathon training and increased activity are risk factors for thrombotic events, and patient reports some transient leg swelling/indentation. However absence of calf pain, persistent swelling, warmth, or systemic signs makes this less likely."
    ],
    "I": [
      "Reactive synovitis / overuse inflammatory bursitis (e.g., prepatellar or pes anserine bursitis): Overuse from increased running can cause local inflammatory fluid and transient swelling; patient reported swelling after first long run that improved with ice.",
      "Septic arthritis / infected bursitis: Would cause acute swelling, heat, systemic symptoms and significant pain — included for completeness but unlikely given absence of fever, redness or systemic illness."
    ],
    "N": [
      "Intra-articular cartilage lesion / loose body (small osteochondral fragment): Can cause intermittent clicking and activity-related pain if a fragment or chondral lesion impinges during motion. No systemic features or imaging yet."
    ],
    "D": [
      "Patellofemoral pain syndrome (chondromalacia patellae / runner's knee): Overuse-related degeneration/irritation of the patellofemoral joint is common in runners, causing anterior knee pain that worsens with running and with repeated flexion/extension; clicking can be present.",
      "Tendinopathy (patellar tendonitis): Overuse in runners can cause pain with activity and with extension from flexion; may present without swelling and with localized pain."
    ],
    "I2": [
      "Medication-related effects (NSAID masking infection/inflammation): Frequent NSAID use could reduce overt inflammatory signs, potentially masking severity. Also occasional recreational drug use (cannabis) unlikely to explain localized knee pain but noted."
    ],
    "C": [
      "Anatomic/patellar maltracking (congenital trochlear dysplasia or patellar alta): Pre-existing patellar tracking issues can predispose to overuse patellofemoral pain and clicking with extension; relevant because pain and clicking occur with flexion/extension."
    ],
    "A": [
      "Early inflammatory arthritis (monoarticular flare such as gout/pseudogout): Can present with acute monoarticular swelling and pain; less likely given gradual onset with activity, lack of severe acute inflammatory signs, and age/clinical context."
    ],
    "T": [
      "Meniscal tear or post-traumatic cartilage injury from prior hockey fall: History of a fall months ago could have produced cartilage or meniscal injury that is now symptomatic with increased running; meniscal injuries can cause clicking and activity-related pain.",
      "Ligamentous injury (ACL/PCL sprain): Prior trauma could have injured ligaments, but classic features (instability/giving way, significant swelling at time of injury) are largely absent now."
    ],
    "E": [
      "Peripheral neuropathy / exertional nerve compression (peroneal nerve irritation or transient neurapraxia): Could explain single episode of toe tingling after exertion; exercise-induced swelling or tight footwear can cause transient paresthesia.",
      "Metabolic bone/joint disease (early osteoarthritis): Age 45 and repetitive loading may cause early degenerative changes but the subacute, activity-linked onset after increasing mileage favors overuse injury rather than chronic OA."
    ]
  },
